<h2>A publishing family divided</h2>
<p>The suit concerns the legacy of a legal publishing house. The plaintiff, widow of Subhash Sagar and daughter-in-law of R.G. Sagar, alleged that defendants 1 to 4 operate "The Delhi Law House" from the premises of the family firm Delhi Law House, using its domain and email identities, and sell her scheduled titles without licence. The defendants answered with a battery of objections: the authors own the copyright absent assignment; the wills are disputed and unproved; a 2016 reconstitution deed released the patriarch\'s interests; and the suit faced a Section 12A Commercial Courts Act pre-institution mediation objection besides.</p>

<h2>The statutory spine of the decision</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Provision</th><th>What it requires</th><th>How it applied</th></tr>
<tr><td>Section 17, Copyright Act</td><td>The author is the first owner of copyright</td><td>The plaintiff is not an author of any of the 47 works</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sections 18–19</td><td>Assignment must be in writing, signed by the assignor</td><td>No assignment from the authors — or from Law Book Company to R.G. Sagar — was produced</td></tr>
<tr><td>Succession principles</td><td>A will passes only what the testator held</td><td>Neither testator being an author or proved assignee, the wills could not transmit copyright</td></tr>
<tr><td>Order XXXIX CPC</td><td>Prima facie case, balance of convenience, irreparable injury</td><td>All three failed; the injunction was refused</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why <em>Midas Hygiene</em> did not rescue the application</h2>
<p>Plaintiffs in IP suits habitually invoke the principle that injunctions follow infringement almost as of course. The court\'s response here is instructive: that principle operates <em>after</em> ownership is shown, not as a substitute for showing it. Likewise, the precedent in which an author\'s own will transmitted copyright was distinguished — the testamentary route works when the testator is the author or a proved assignee, and fails when the will is asked to conjure a title the testator never held. The judgment is a reminder that in copyright litigation, the chain of title is examined link by link, and the first missing link ends the inquiry.</p>

<h2>Lessons for publishing and family businesses</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Paper the assignments contemporaneously: every commissioned work, revision and edition should carry a written, signed assignment or an employment framework under Section 17. Keep the corporate and family layers separate — publishing from a firm\'s premises under a similar name creates exactly the goodwill entanglement this suit displays. Where title rests on succession, obtain probate or letters of administration before suing on the will. And assess the remedy realistically: where the grievance is fundamentally about revenue share, courts will steer the dispute towards accounts and damages rather than mid-stream injunctions.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The findings are expressly prima facie: authorship, the assignments, the wills, the 1962 certificate, the 2016 reconstitution deed and the defences all remain open for trial. The order decides only that the plaintiff\'s interim case did not meet the Order XXXIX threshold.</p>
</div>
